// Which of the ways you may walk on are nicer to walk on. Stacked after
// fm_foot.json or fm_hike.json by all four pedestrian profiles; never by a
// vehicle. Nothing references it -- custom_model_files in the config is what
// stacks it.
//
// Its own file because both bases want exactly this ladder and nothing about it
// is specific to either. Separate from foot_temporal.json for the opposite
// reason: that one takes ways out of the graph, this one only ranks them.
//
// The problem, from Laci. Where sidewalks are mapped as separate footways rather
// than as sidewalk=* on the road, the road centreline is shorter and straighter
// than the sidewalk with its detours via crossings, so the router puts
// pedestrians on the road. Reproduced at Repašského, Bratislava,
// 48.189755/17.032993 -> 48.190370/17.036051: the route ran footway x12 ->
// tertiary -> residential -> footway. With this ladder it is 267 m of footway
// throughout, 9 m longer.
//
// A penalty, not a ban: in a village with no mapped sidewalk the carriageway is
// the only way through, and a ban would answer "Connection between locations not
// found" instead of routing.
//
// Only decreases, never a boost. Raising footway instead works just as well --
// x1.1 fixes the junction and a 12.7 km rural route does not move until x1.5 --
// but a model that only multiplies by <= 1 keeps the shape a query custom model
// is allowed to have (FindMinMax.checkLMConstraints caps those at 1), so these
// rules could later be sent per request against an LM profile unchanged.
//
// Measured, not guessed:
//
//   tertiary 0.7    junction fixed   rural foot 15718 m   +23.8 %
//   tertiary 0.8    junction fixed   rural foot 13387 m   +5.4 %
//   tertiary 0.9    junction fixed   rural foot 12698 m   unchanged
//
// 0.95 rather than 0.9 because of hiking: both fix the junction, but on a 12.6 km
// rural hike route 0.9 costs +2.4 km (+19 %) and 0.95 costs 4 m. The junction
// never needed the stronger value. An earlier attempt -- a flat 0.35 across seven
// classes -- also fixed the junction but cost +22.7 % on a rural foot route, and
// was worse in kind: it pushed the route off footway and residential onto
// secondary, because penalising a housing street as hard as a trunk road leaves
// the trunk road looking no worse.
//
// living_street is absent deliberately: shared space where pedestrians have right
// of way and no sidewalk is expected. So are path, track and steps, which keep
// what foot_priority gives them (1.2) -- a hiker would rather take a path than a
// tertiary road, and 1.2 against 0.95 is what makes that happen.
//
// Effective values, foot_priority x this file:
//   footway, pedestrian, path, track, steps, living_street   1.20
//   residential 1.14   service 1.08   unclassified 1.00
//   tertiary 0.95 (0.76 if sidewalk=no|none|separate)
//   secondary 0.56 (0.35)   primary 0.40 (0.25)
//
// The bracketed values are the ones GraphHopper should be using far more often
// than it does: FootPriorityParser reads only the legacy `sidewalk` key, so
// `sidewalk:both=separate` -- now the more common spelling in Europe -- is
// invisible and the road is promoted two rungs instead of demoted. That is the
// actual cause of Laci's junction; this ladder compensates for it. See
// graphhopper/graphhopper#3042.
{
  "priority": [
    { "if":      "road_class == PRIMARY",     "multiply_by": "0.5" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == SECONDARY",   "multiply_by": "0.7" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == TERTIARY",    "multiply_by": "0.95" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == RESIDENTIAL", "multiply_by": "0.95" },
    { "else_if": "road_class == SERVICE",     "multiply_by": "0.9" }
  ]
}
